  "id": "DrugBank_DB13873 - MONDO_1807",
  "verbalization": "Fenofibric Acid is implicated in the treatment of Familial Hyperlipidemia, a condition that is linked to Obsolete Familial Combined Hyperlipidemia through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Fenofibric Acid may act as a secondary therapeutic agent in the management of Familial Hyperlipidemia, which in turn is associated with Obsolete Familial Combined Hyperlipidemia. This relationship is supported by the shared indication of Familial Hyperlipidemia with multiple drugs, including Lovastatin and Niacin, which are also linked to Obsolete Familial Combined Hyperlipidemia. The integration of these pathways implies a broader mechanism of action, possibly involving lipid metabolism regulation, that could explain the observed relationship. The presence of multiple overlapping indications suggests that Fenofibric Acid may contribute to the pathophysiology of Obsolete Familial Combined Hyperlipidemia through its effects on lipid homeostasis, although the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
